From: pi <pi@local>
Subject: [PATCH] memory: recycle romd FlatView variants and flush the TLB by
 changed range

A ROM device (pmb887x NOR flash, pflash) toggles romd_mode on every
command: array->command mode on the first command write, back to array
mode on READ_ARRAY.  Every toggle is a memory topology change, so the
default commit path throws away *every* FlatView and re-renders all of
them from scratch - dominated by the phys-page radix refill of the
dispatch tree over the full flash (a 64 MB flash at 4k granularity =
16k page inserts).  The firmware flips up to a few hundred times per
second while boot-polling flash status: on the wasm/TCI build that is
~18k full re-renders per boot, 3.9 s spent inside
memory_region_transaction_commit (measured with a temporary
ns-accumulator counter, since removed), plus one full tlb_flush() per
flip through the tcg commit listener - which also throws away every
unrelated code/data translation and pays a page-table walk per page to
get them back (~33 TLB refills per flip measured).

Two mechanisms fix this, and they only win together:

1. system/memory.c: the render is a pure function of the MR-tree
   state, and romd_mode is the only MR field these commits change.  A
   FlatView is therefore tagged with the state it was rendered from -
   (topo_gen, romd_sig): topo_gen is bumped on every non-romd topology
   change (any other mutation - subregion add/del, resize, enable,
   readonly, alias, finalize - flows through a commit that takes the
   default path), romd_sig identifies the set of ROM-device MRs
   currently in command mode (every other ROM device is in array mode,
   the init default).  A commit whose only change is romd_mode adopts
   the previously rendered variant of the same (root, tag) from a
   16-slot stash instead of re-rendering; roots whose current view
   already carries the new tag are skipped entirely (their tree did
   not change).  Reuse correctness needs exactly the two tags to
   match.

2. The tcg commit listener flushes the TLB by *changed physical
   range* instead of dropping everything: the listener's
   region_add/region_del callbacks record the sections the topology
   pass diffed, and the flush drops only entries translating into one
   of those ranges (cputlb.c tlb_flush_phys_ranges).  Entries outside
   the changed ranges translate to the same section content as before
   - including entries installed against a previous FlatView variant
   that mechanism 1 recycles - so they stay valid across the toggle.

Lifetime safety for kept entries that reference a *previous* view's
dispatch: every view TLB entries can reference is either current or in
the variant stash, and a stash slot is only ever dropped from
memory_region_transaction_commit's listener phase, where
memory_topology_views_recycled() latches "a variant was evicted" and
the commit's flush falls back to the full tlb_flush() - before the
vCPU can re-enter execution and before RCU can reclaim the view (the
flush never dereferences the entries' sections, it only memsets
them).

Counters (wasm-diag.h): romdFlip/topoCommit/topoReuse are readable via
wasm_memstat() and tools/memstat.mjs.

Measured on the wasm build (S75 fullflash, -icount shift=3,sleep=off,
interleaved A/B against the previous build; window = wall secs of
deterministic guest work between v=2 and v=7, lower is better):
  - topology commit time: 3857 ms -> 421 ms per boot-so-far (-89%),
    30894 FlatView-variant reuses (topoReuse)
  - v-window: baseline 25.1-28.3 s -> 22.5-25.1 s (8/8 interleaved
    pairs won; with the final counter set every candidate run beat
    every baseline run: 22.5/23.2 vs 25.2/25.3)
  - guest insns @110 s: +4-9 %; run-to-run variance collapsed (the
    re-render bursts were the runs that suffered under host load)
  - idle screen (v=245) ~160 s wall (previous builds ~185 s); soak to
    v=827 with growing LCD updates, no >>EXIT<<; LCD pixel-diff vs the
    reference idle screen equal to the previous build (drift = clock +
    auto keyboard lock only)
Native (real TCG JIT) is structurally unaffected: the same commit
counts apply, but a re-render costs microseconds there, and the full
TLB flush is only replaced by a strictly smaller flush.  Native suite
4/4 PASS (s75/el71/c81/ke800, tests/run.mjs --label romd-variants).

This closes "remaining opportunity #3 (flash romd topology churn,
~4-5 % of vCPU)" from doc/optimization-playbook.md - and refutes its
risk assessment note: with the variant cache the semantics stay
stock (full flush whenever anything but romd flags changed or a
variant was evicted).

diff --git a/accel/tcg/cputlb.c b/accel/tcg/cputlb.c
--- a/accel/tcg/cputlb.c	2026-09-09 19:59:33.378778879 +0000
+++ b/accel/tcg/cputlb.c	2026-09-09 18:34:02.764370145 +0000
@@ -493,6 +493,80 @@
     return tlb_flush_entry_mask_locked(tlb_entry, page, -1);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Selective variant of tlb_flush() for memory topology commits
+ * (tcg_commit): drop only the entries that translate into one of the
+ * changed physical ranges [lo[i], hi[i]) reported by the commit's
+ * region_add/region_del listener callbacks.
+ *
+ * A full tlb_flush() per commit would also throw away every unrelated
+ * code/data translation and pay a page-table walk per page to get them
+ * back; ROM devices flip their romd mode per flash command, which
+ * re-flushed the whole TLB tens of thousands of times per boot (each
+ * flip followed by a ~33-entry refill storm of the running code).
+ *
+ * Entries outside the changed ranges translate to the same section
+ * content as before, so they stay valid - including entries installed
+ * against a previous FlatView variant that a romd commit recycled
+ * (system/memory.c keeps the variants alive; their dispatches and
+ * sections are only freed after tlb_flush() semantics would have
+ * dropped them, see tcg_commit's full-flush fallback).
+ */
+void tlb_flush_phys_ranges(CPUState *cpu,
+                           const hwaddr *lo, const hwaddr *hi,
+                           unsigned n)
+{
+    int mmu_idx;
+
+    assert_cpu_is_self(cpu);
+
+    qemu_spin_lock(&cpu->neg.tlb.c.lock);
+
+    for (mmu_idx = 0; mmu_idx < NB_MMU_MODES; mmu_idx++) {
+        CPUTLBDesc *desc = &cpu->neg.tlb.d[mmu_idx];
+        CPUTLBDescFast *fast = cpu_tlb_fast(cpu, mmu_idx);
+        size_t nr = (fast->mask >> CPU_TLB_ENTRY_BITS) + 1;
+        size_t i;
+        unsigned r;
+
+        for (i = 0; i < nr; i++) {
+            CPUTLBEntry *te = &fast->table[i];
+            hwaddr pa;
+
+            if (tlb_entry_is_empty(te)) {
+                continue;
+            }
+            pa = desc->fulltlb[i].phys_addr;
+            for (r = 0; r < n; r++) {
+                if (pa >= lo[r] && pa < hi[r]) {
+                    memset(te, -1, sizeof(*te));
+                    tlb_n_used_entries_dec(cpu, mmu_idx);
+                    break;
+                }
+            }
+        }
+        for (i = 0; i < CPU_VTLB_SIZE; i++) {
+            CPUTLBEntry *te = &desc->vtable[i];
+            hwaddr pa;
+
+            if (tlb_entry_is_empty(te)) {
+                continue;
+            }
+            pa = desc->vfulltlb[i].phys_addr;
+            for (r = 0; r < n; r++) {
+                if (pa >= lo[r] && pa < hi[r]) {
+                    memset(te, -1, sizeof(*te));
+                    tlb_n_used_entries_dec(cpu, mmu_idx);
+                    break;
+                }
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
+    qemu_spin_unlock(&cpu->neg.tlb.c.lock);
+}
+
+
 /* Called with tlb_c.lock held */
 static void tlb_flush_vtlb_page_mask_locked(CPUState *cpu, int mmu_idx,
                                             vaddr page,
